00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:21.250
I think a lot of people measure and don't measure just the system, but I think

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.290
what a lot of people measure are the results of the system.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.450
We have test pass results and code coverage and deployment rate.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.770
People get all excited about the Dora metrics.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:31.970
This drives me crazy.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.490
And again, they're very good metrics.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.810
And those are, you know, I've been out of it for so long.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:36.930
I forgot what they are.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:37.410
I don't care.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:38.690
I do care.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:47.300
Deployment velocity, mean time to recovery, things like that, which

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.220
companies after Accelerate came out.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:49.860
Wonderful book.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.740
Teams clamored to build dashboards that automatically measured these things.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:03.070
Yet forgot that what those measurements are, those measurements occur and improve

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.350
when the system improves.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.550
Measurements are just like tools for maybe helping you doing the right thing.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:14.270
Right.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:15.070
People get stuck.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.030
They want to build an automated dashboard for everything.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.630
And what they forgot is, or what they, and so many people,

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.540
man, I've been spending time on LinkedIn and it's full of so many fricking idiots.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:30.460
And for years, people were like, we're going to measure these metrics.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.580
They've never read the book, the book, and they don't realize those metrics

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:37.500
came from surveys, not from trying to put a dashboard in place to measure

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.460
these things and deploying faster doesn't make your software higher quality.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.740
You can, for any of those things you can measure, you can make shortcuts.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:48.780
So you want to measure the system.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:54.620
So the thing I talked about with Tim a little bit was I tracked a bunch of

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.620
metrics on product delivery and estimates.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.420
And I use that.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.620
We use that to learn continuously and to track improvements and things.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.380
And we got some insights, which is pretty cool.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.440
So go ahead.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.400
You're going to ask a question.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.080
What kind of things did you track then?

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.840
Maybe I didn't follow everything.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:10.080
Okay.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.700
I tracked, so in summary, because I realized not all three listeners

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.900
listen to every episode.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.940
One of the things I'll back up a bit.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.420
When I lead teams, I expect I don't have scrum masters.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.060
I think it's a, and I'm sorry if you're a scrum master today,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.340
but it's, I think it's a dying role or unnecessary role.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:33.460
I believe engineering leads need to own their project management

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.430
and they need to learn how to do it because they're going to need

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:38.750
to learn how to do that as they become engineering managers,

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:42.910
engineering directors and engineering VPs and engineering, whatever, after that.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.110
They need to learn how to get their teams to deliver.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:47.790
I don't care what they use.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.390
I don't care if it's scrum.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.620
By the way, scrum was invented before the Agile Manifesto.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.140
People just wedged it in there to make it work.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.180
And most people use scrum.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.380
Tim and I talked about this.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.660
Most people use scrum to pretend that they're doing Agile

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.620
without actually being Agile, which is adaptive, not iterative.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.500
You were mentioning safe as this kind of wrapper around scrum.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.860
Oh, and safe is just horrible.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.340
The vomit is coming up in my mouth as I talk about it.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:20.420
So what I do is I require, I ask my dev leads to give estimates

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:21.540
on everything they deliver.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.060
When is this project going to be done?

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.750
And again, predicting the future is impossible,

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.190
but we're trying to give the best guess.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.630
So I had to use confidence levels.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:34.190
So give me a date that you have a 50% confidence in.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.480
And what I found in years of experimenting is that 50% is a sweet spot

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:41.920
between putting some decent planning into place

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.520
and completely guessing on what you're doing.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:51.020
I go over this stuff weekly with the leads.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:55.980
And what I track is the day their initial estimate date

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.980
and the actual date they deliver.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.500
So I can get deltas between the date something's actually done

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.020
and the day they thought it would be done.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.260
And the short story is things that I predicted

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.460
to be right around two weeks, from 12 to 16 days,

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:11.330
they were correct 80% of the time.

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:16.030
It's a really good sweet spot for delivery,

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.710
which I think why for teams using Scrum,

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:22.270
why two weeks sprint, gross, works really well.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.270
What I found is anything over four weeks

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.070
was all over the map, all over the map.

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.270
Almost all of them were late.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.950
A few were extremely early

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.390
because they thought it was going to be four weeks

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.070
and ended up being like three days.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.030
But for the most part,

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:39.470
anything four weeks and beyond impossible to estimate.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.350
And this is across an entire team of about 300 engineers.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.870
Now you're just about measuring

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:47.950
how good they are about estimating.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.190
What is the intended change in behavior?

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.670
The goal is I want them to get better at estimating.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.430
Again, I balance transparency.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.710
If they read my newsletters,

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:02.750
transparency, psychological safety, and accountability.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.590
And by being accountable for dates.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.350
And also when they get a date wrong,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.190
we have a weekly meeting to discuss these things.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.750
And they say, gonna need to move the date out

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.190
on this project by four days.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.590
And at first I say, great.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.430
What did you learn and why?

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.350
And they'll tell me, oh, I forgot to calculate

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:21.310
for an employee being out of the office.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.470
Or we had a dependency on team X

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.310
and they're behind because of A, B, and C.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:25.950
And I can ask,

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.190
how could you plan for better for that in the future?

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.590
I said, we can have probably a shorter conversation

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.190
upfront with that team will work better.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:36.030
A little bit, just a teeny little 20 second retro

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:36.990
if a date needs to change.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.510
And over time, when I ask that question,

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.790
they'll say, our project is moving out five days

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:46.830
because we failed to anticipate this.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.510
And to me to get this in the future,

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:49.230
we're going to do this.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:51.870
That's the learning I want to see.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.430
So they get better over time because I don't care

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:55.070
if they're wrong.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:58.190
We're humans, we make mistakes.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:02.270
What I want them to do is make a mistake,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.350
learn from it, and get better the next time.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:04.850
And they do.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.620
I wonder if this will be misunderstood.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.260
And as you as last time said,

