Someone emailed me this week

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:23.890
and told me my link was dead

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:25.410
and it was because I forgot to renew it.

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:27.810
So I had to go update the web page, but I forgot.

00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.170
And so far, so I'll get to it.

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:32.980
Slack group.

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:34.580
You can ask questions about the hike.

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:35.620
It's all the stats I have.

00:26:36.340 --> 00:26:38.900
They're interesting and also not interesting

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:41.220
because there's nothing remarkable in there

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.210
other than I walked a whole bunch of miles.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:45.810
But you can also ask Spy

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:48.370
about general management quality,

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:50.930
people leadership things and the blog posts

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:52.290
and the newsletter, I think,

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.290
was what you wanted to talk about it, right?

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:56.450
Yeah, I got nothing to do.

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:57.490
I got nothing to do.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:59.890
So I've been writing.

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:02.130
I'll talk a little bit more about writing in a second.

00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:03.730
But one of the things I wrote recently,

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:06.530
and I think you had some tangential comments

00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:08.290
or tangential ideas to this,

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:09.970
is my latest newsletter

00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:11.570
is about quality being a system.

00:27:12.770 --> 00:27:14.290
And this goes back to you measure

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:16.290
the results of the system, not the things.

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:19.570
What Brent and I have talked about a lot

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:23.970
is teams are so ultra focused on testing

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:27.280
that they don't measure quality.

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:31.430
And I don't really care about testing.

00:27:31.430 --> 00:27:34.230
It needs to happen, but I care about quality.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:35.910
And one of the things I called out this blog post,

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:37.030
actually the main thing I called out

00:27:37.030 --> 00:27:39.830
is quality is a result of the system.

00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:41.030
If you build a quality system,

00:27:42.710 --> 00:27:44.710
odds are you're going to build a quality product.

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:46.550
Had a little pushback on that in the comments.

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:49.670
Someone said systems don't have quality.

00:27:49.670 --> 00:27:50.950
Only things have quality.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.910
And I disagree.

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:56.550
I mean, yes, things have quality.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:00.470
But you don't generally produce a quality thing

00:28:00.470 --> 00:28:01.350
from a bad system.

00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:04.960
And a bad system can produce a quality thing,

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.000
but not consistently.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:08.720
So, and I will stand by that.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:09.360
And it's not like,

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.200
and I was accused of redefining quality,

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:12.960
but I just used piercings

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:14.560
from Zen and the Art of Motorcycle Maintenance's

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:17.280
explanation of quality,

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:19.600
which is a philosophy of quality,

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:22.480
but not maybe not quality in the way

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:25.680
traditional testers view quality.

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.870
So, I'm curious.

00:28:28.870 --> 00:28:29.830
Yeah, well, I guess, I guess.

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:30.390
Yeah, go ahead.

00:28:30.390 --> 00:28:31.190
Traditional testers,

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:34.520
I would say good testers do care

00:28:34.520 --> 00:28:36.760
about the way of thinking about quality

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:39.880
in terms of attention and care, right?

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:41.560
That's one of the main points,

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:44.280
taking care and attention to things.

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:46.920
And that's totally in the testers' wheelhouse, right?

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:51.920
But it is.

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.030
But I, a lot of,

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:57.990
and I'm curious in your take on this is

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.350
I think a lot of testers,

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:01.910
and again, my view is not talking to folks like you.

00:29:01.910 --> 00:29:04.470
We have a nice little almost echo chamber

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:05.910
on the modern testing slack,

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:07.990
people who get that quality as a system

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:10.150
and they get that we have to build quality in

00:29:10.150 --> 00:29:13.430
as a result of behaviors and practices,

00:29:13.430 --> 00:29:14.790
not heroics.

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:17.430
But when I go to LinkedIn to look at jobs,

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:20.470
I see posts from people talking about

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:24.470
just the dumbest things about how testers work.

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:26.630
And it's just, it's so 1999 to me.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:34.180
So you had something, I forget, I've already forgotten.

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:35.700
I should have it here in front of me.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:37.700
But let me ask you a question

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:39.060
if I can find my notes here.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:45.650
Tell me about your thoughts on

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.410
regarding bad quality and bad luck.

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:50.870
Hey, it's kind of related to your newsletter, really.

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:52.230
I thought so.

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:53.510
That's why I brought it up here.

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:57.830
It's been recently, I think, maybe,

00:29:57.830 --> 00:30:00.470
yeah, maybe AI has played a role.

00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:03.990
Let me start differently.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:10.290
Do you know persons who run into trouble

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.610
more often than others, seemingly?

00:30:12.610 --> 00:30:17.060
Like I have people in my vicinity who have,

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:20.260
they always come up with stories.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:22.100
Like I was trying to book like a flight

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.340
and I got like this trouble.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:25.940
I had like customer support issues

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:30.290
and always like some folks who are always finding

00:30:31.430 --> 00:30:33.750
and dealing with issues in their life.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:36.740
We used to call them bug magnets.

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:41.410
And I was wondering like if bug magnets are just,

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:43.890
I don't know, yeah, okay, bug magnets.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:46.690
I seem to observe bug magnets.

00:30:47.410 --> 00:30:48.610
If they really exist, I don't know,

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:50.290
but I observe bug magnets.

00:30:50.290 --> 00:30:51.090
People seem to...

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:53.760
I have thoughts.

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:58.480
I think it could be partially true.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:00.160
I think it comes from a couple different things

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:02.000
and they're actually at odds with each other.

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:06.280
Some people just notice things more closely.

00:31:06.280 --> 00:31:07.400
Like, oh, this is off.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.760
I'm gonna make, or this seems wrong.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:13.160
And they're not necessarily a magnet.

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:14.360
They're good observers.

00:31:14.360 --> 00:31:17.480
And some people, the magnets get,

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:22.440
there are these folks on LinkedIn that drive me nuts.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:24.440
They're kind of magnets

00:31:24.440 --> 00:31:26.520
because they're great noticers of things,

00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:29.640
but they also notice very trivial mundane things

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:32.760
that don't matter to 99.9% of the users.

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:35.400
But they dwell on them and talk about,

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:38.120
and then they eventually, this is what drives me crazy.

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:41.960
They eventually evolve into blaming poor testing

00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:44.840
or poor management or AI or automation

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:47.400
or whatever for the fact that these things

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:48.440
they noticed are broken.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:51.000
I'm not gonna call people out

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:53.480
and I don't follow this person,

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.640
but I follow enough people that add comments

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:57.720
to their posts that they show up on my feed,

00:31:57.720 --> 00:32:00.760
but they're constantly talking about the bugs they found

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:02.840
and how software is horrible.

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:04.600
And there are things that just don't matter.

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:06.520
So the bug magnets notice these things.

00:32:08.150 --> 00:32:09.990
There's another end of things.

00:32:09.990 --> 00:32:11.350
And I'll tell a story about this

00:32:11.350 --> 00:32:12.550
from when I was at Microsoft.

00:32:15.350 --> 00:32:18.630
On Windows 98 and beyond,

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:19.830
the consumer version of Windows,

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:23.350
I did a lot of app compatibility work,

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:25.030
trying to figure out why apps weren't working.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:29.730
I worked on the three core components of Windows

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:31.730
at the time, the graphical designer interface,

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:35.330
user interface, GDI, user, and kernel,

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.250
which were, and kernel was actually,

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:39.090
I won't go into the details.

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:40.370
This is how the app,

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:44.610
this is the main libraries applications were built on.

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:45.330
I'll just say that.

00:32:46.050 --> 00:32:47.970
And there would be a tester.

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:51.010
And I say, hey, this,

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:52.850
it looks like this application shouldn't work

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:54.130
because we had lots of data on stuff

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:55.330
and how they called APIs.

00:32:56.130 --> 00:32:57.410
Can you show me how you've been testing this?

00:32:57.410 --> 00:32:58.690
Make sure we can figure out what's going on.

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:02.850
And they'd walk through and they would,

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:06.290
like this obvious error would come up

00:33:06.290 --> 00:33:07.650
and they click dismiss and keep going.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:09.090
I said, hold on, wait a minute.

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:10.130
What's going on?

00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:11.570
They said, I said, what do you mean?

00:33:11.570 --> 00:33:13.010
I'm trying to show you how I test this.

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:15.890
But there was that error and you just skipped over it.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:16.770
That comes up all the time.

00:33:16.770 --> 00:33:17.410
So I don't worry about it.

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:20.130
I said, no, you need to tell me.

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:24.050
And there are a class of folks

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:26.530
who notice these things barely,

00:33:26.530 --> 00:33:30.770
but are able to work around them so easily.

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:32.370
And they're not all so obvious,

00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:35.330
but they're able to work around these errors

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:38.610
in a way that makes it seem to their subconscious

00:33:38.610 --> 00:33:39.570
at least doesn't exist.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:43.280
So I think a lot of folks kind of live in between.

00:33:44.070 --> 00:33:46.790
I mean, all software has bugs, all software.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:50.550
And if you don't agree, yeah, all software has bugs.

00:33:52.900 --> 00:33:54.740
I think the majority of people,

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:57.540
and again, also whether it's a bug,

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:59.700
whether it's an actual bug or something is broken

00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:03.860
or it's just not intuitive or anything in between,

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:04.660
it all exists.

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:08.050
Some people are really good

00:34:08.050 --> 00:34:12.530
at subconsciously working around the non-intuitiveness.

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:14.930
You can look at, I mean, Photoshop,

00:34:14.930 --> 00:34:17.250
super powerful software, super difficult to use.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:18.370
Is it buggy?

00:34:18.370 --> 00:34:20.370
Well, a little bit, but is it useful?

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:20.930
Hell yes.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:24.200
And people have learned to work around those things so well

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:26.120
that it's provided them value,

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:28.680
which according to Weinberg is quality.

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:32.900
I think some folks get so caught up

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:35.540
in every single thing not working

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:38.850
that they are a bug magnet,

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:41.650
but they're a bug noticer more than a bug magnet.

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:43.890
And they get stuck.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:47.810
It's like when someone who's neurodivergent

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:48.690
sees something wrong

00:34:48.690 --> 00:34:50.530
and they just can't stop thinking about it

00:34:50.530 --> 00:34:52.450
to the fact they can't move on with their work.

00:34:53.090 --> 00:34:55.090
I think some testers are like that

00:34:55.090 --> 00:34:57.730
and said some companies for some project is very valuable.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:04.020
I don't know if most users operate like that.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:05.540
When I look at the software I use,

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.340
I had a team's call this week for a job interview.

00:35:09.380 --> 00:35:11.060
Teams is a horrible piece of software

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:12.500
and I can say that having worked on it.

00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:15.960
But I can make it work.

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:17.080
I know how to work around it.

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:20.040
I don't sit there and talk about how awful it is

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:22.040
other than this podcast

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:25.540
because it works more or less for what I need it to do.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:28.230
Is it intuitive?

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:28.950
No.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:30.310
Is it bug free?

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:30.710
No.

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:31.590
Is it seamless?

00:35:31.590 --> 00:35:31.830
No.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:32.790
Is it easy to use?

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:33.290
No.

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:34.870
But I can make it work.

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:36.470
I'm not going to go post on LinkedIn

00:35:36.470 --> 00:35:38.550
about how this and this and this

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:39.670
don't make sense for the user

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:40.550
and this and this and this

00:35:40.550 --> 00:35:43.670
because the work around is so easy

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:47.430
that I can use it even though it sucks.

00:35:47.430 --> 00:35:49.270
And I think probably most people

00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:50.150
live in that area.

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:55.430
Maybe it's been conditioned by decades of bad software

00:35:55.990 --> 00:36:00.390
but I think people just tend to work around these things

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:02.390
which is not great for testers.

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:04.950
You do want testers who notice those things

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:08.070
but, and I'll tie this into a future subject

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:09.190
after I let you talk for a second

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:11.830
but I think testers, too many testers

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:13.750
and too many testers who people follow

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:17.430
get stuck in the fact that bugs exist

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:22.100
and lose fact that not all bugs are created equal

00:36:22.100 --> 00:36:24.660
and only a subset of those actually impact

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.260
customer usage, customer acquisition

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:27.460
customer attention, etc.

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:30.760
I could kind of turn around and say maybe

00:36:30.760 --> 00:36:32.760
I don't know if the software is getting worse

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.240
if that's like old man yelling at cloud things

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:38.600
or like software getting worse

00:36:38.600 --> 00:36:40.600
I don't know with AI maybe

00:36:40.600 --> 00:36:41.960
the error margins are getting bigger

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:47.080
I don't but you are not like the 99

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:53.480
like I wonder if you're producing so much bad software

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:57.560
